1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:08,348
There are two versions of this story,
and they have opposite answers.

2
00:00:08,388 --> 00:00:11,673
To clone is to make your own
copy of a repository with Git.

3
00:00:12,013 --> 00:00:15,093
In the first, the intern
typed git clone with a URL.

4
00:00:15,133 --> 00:00:20,013
What Git ran on the laptop because of that:
nothing the repository's author chose.

5
00:00:20,353 --> 00:00:24,655
In the second, the footnote linked to
a tarball, an archive file, called a

6
00:00:24,695 --> 00:00:29,466
"reproduction package", and the intern
unpacked it and changed into the directory.

7
00:00:29,506 --> 00:00:33,045
The shell prompt showed the
branch name, as shell prompts do.

8
00:00:33,085 --> 00:00:37,974
To show it, the prompt ran git status. And
git status read a configuration file that

9
00:00:38,014 --> 00:00:39,558
the package's author wrote.

10
00:00:39,898 --> 00:00:44,647
Same repository, same content, same intern.
The difference is how the .git directory

11
00:00:44,687 --> 00:00:47,555
got onto the disk. Keep the
second version in mind.

12
00:00:47,595 --> 00:00:50,234
You'll watch it happen,
harmlessly, in the lab.

13
00:00:52,074 --> 00:00:55,974
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

14
00:00:56,014 --> 00:01:01,334
This video opens Module 30, repository
security, and it starts on your own machine.

15
00:01:01,374 --> 00:01:04,886
For two modules the question
was what runs on a runner.

16
00:01:04,926 --> 00:01:10,052
Now the question is what runs on your
laptop, with your SSH agent and your cloud

17
00:01:10,092 --> 00:01:11,409
credentials in reach.

18
00:01:11,749 --> 00:01:17,655
Everything in this video is Git, not GitHub.
Three topics, in the order of sections

19
00:01:17,695 --> 00:01:21,667
21B.2 to 21B.4. What a clone
copies and what it doesn't.

20
00:01:21,707 --> 00:01:26,323
The three standing guards that Git
added in 2022: safe.directory,

21
00:01:26,363 --> 00:01:32,270
safe.bareRepository and protocol.file.allow.
And the places where "cloning is safe"

22
00:01:32,310 --> 00:01:38,287
has had exceptions: recursive clones. Plus
what hooks can and can't do as a control,

23
00:01:38,327 --> 00:01:39,792
and what stars prove.

24
00:01:40,132 --> 00:01:45,160
The demonstration uses a repository prepared
by "somebody else" that contains a hook

25
00:01:45,200 --> 00:01:50,229
and an alias. A hook is a file that Git runs
at a defined point of a command, and an

26
00:01:50,269 --> 00:01:55,056
alias is a short name you define for a
command. Both are harmless here: the hook

27
00:01:55,096 --> 00:01:58,797
appends a line to a log file,
and the alias prints a sentence.

28
00:01:58,837 --> 00:02:01,995
They stand for what a hostile
author could put there.

29
00:02:02,035 --> 00:02:03,503
Nothing hostile is shown.

30
00:02:05,343 --> 00:02:06,921
Here's what you'll walk away with.

31
00:02:07,261 --> 00:02:08,674
After this video you can:

32
00:02:09,014 --> 00:02:13,788
explain why cloning an untrusted repository
is generally safe and unpacking an

33
00:02:13,828 --> 00:02:15,146
archive of one is not;

34
00:02:15,486 --> 00:02:20,323
explain "dubious ownership" and what
safe.directory does and should not be set to;

35
00:02:20,663 --> 00:02:25,801
say what safe.bareRepository=explicit
refuses and which attack it answers;

36
00:02:26,141 --> 00:02:31,022
explain protocol.file.allow and the risk
of a recursive clone of an untrusted

37
00:02:31,062 --> 00:02:31,725
repository;

38
00:02:32,065 --> 00:02:34,900
say what stars and popularity
do and do not prove.

39
00:02:36,740 --> 00:02:41,772
In one sentence. Cloning a repository copies
its objects and refs and nothing that

40
00:02:41,812 --> 00:02:45,483
Git would execute, so cloning
and reading is generally safe.

41
00:02:45,523 --> 00:02:49,627
Running Git inside a .git directory
that somebody else wrote isn't.

42
00:02:49,667 --> 00:02:54,266
Objects are the stored snapshots and
files, and refs are the names, such as

43
00:02:54,306 --> 00:02:56,060
branches, that point at them.

44
00:02:56,400 --> 00:03:01,101
Precisely. Two kinds of file under
.git can make Git execute a program.

45
00:03:01,441 --> 00:03:06,406
Hooks: the files in .git/hooks, or
wherever core.hooksPath points.

46
00:03:06,746 --> 00:03:12,555
And configuration: .git/config. An alias
beginning with an exclamation mark names a

47
00:03:12,595 --> 00:03:18,194
shell command. So do core.pager, core.editor,
core.fsmonitor, core.sshCommand, a

48
00:03:18,234 --> 00:03:22,634
credential helper, a filter driver,
and several other settings.

49
00:03:22,974 --> 00:03:26,294
Git's manual states the rule
in its SECURITY section.

50
00:03:26,334 --> 00:03:29,528
Configuration and hooks
aren't copied by git clone.

51
00:03:29,568 --> 00:03:34,854
So cloning a remote repository with untrusted
content and inspecting it with git log

52
00:03:34,894 --> 00:03:39,926
is generally safe. Running Git commands
in a .git directory, or the working tree

53
00:03:39,966 --> 00:03:43,223
around it, that came from
an untrusted source isn't.

54
00:03:43,263 --> 00:03:47,979
And the documented way to get a clean
copy of such a directory is git clone

55
00:03:48,019 --> 00:03:48,676
--no-local.

56
00:03:49,016 --> 00:03:54,444
Inside .git. A clone creates a new .git
from the template directory of your own Git

57
00:03:54,484 --> 00:03:59,121
installation. Its hooks directory
holds only the inactive sample files.

58
00:03:59,161 --> 00:04:04,655
Its config holds what git clone writes: the
repository format, the origin remote and

59
00:04:04,695 --> 00:04:09,925
the branch's upstream. From the source it
receives objects, in a pack, and refs.

60
00:04:09,965 --> 00:04:11,309
Nothing else crosses.

61
00:04:11,649 --> 00:04:15,434
The three guards. Git added
three standing defenses in 2022.

62
00:04:15,474 --> 00:04:20,663
Each closes one way of getting you to run
Git inside configuration that you didn't

63
00:04:20,703 --> 00:04:21,046
write.

64
00:04:21,386 --> 00:04:25,993
safe.directory. In one sentence: Git
refuses to read the configuration of a

65
00:04:26,033 --> 00:04:31,197
repository whose directory is owned by a
different operating-system user, unless you

66
00:04:31,237 --> 00:04:33,366
have listed that directory as safe.

67
00:04:33,706 --> 00:04:38,592
Why it exists: Git discovers a repository
by walking up from the current directory.

68
00:04:38,632 --> 00:04:43,578
On a shared machine another user can create
a .git directory high up, in a temporary

69
00:04:43,618 --> 00:04:48,089
or scratch directory. Without this check,
any Git command you run below that

70
00:04:48,129 --> 00:04:51,116
directory would load their
configuration and hooks.

71
00:04:51,456 --> 00:04:56,571
The check compares the owner of the repository
directory with the user running Git.

72
00:04:56,611 --> 00:04:59,800
safe.directory lists
exceptions. It is multi-valued.

73
00:04:59,840 --> 00:05:05,017
It accepts a path ending in a star for
everything under a directory, and a lone star

74
00:05:05,057 --> 00:05:10,110
to switch the check off. And it's honored
only in protected configuration: system,

75
00:05:10,150 --> 00:05:14,333
global and command scope. So a
repository can't declare itself safe.

76
00:05:14,673 --> 00:05:19,416
Outdated advice: answers from 2022 add
a star to safe.directory in global

77
00:05:19,456 --> 00:05:24,920
configuration. That switches the protection
off for every repository on the machine.

78
00:05:24,960 --> 00:05:27,868
List the one directory,
or fix the ownership.

79
00:05:28,208 --> 00:05:33,820
safe.bareRepository. In one sentence:
with the value explicit, Git uses a bare

80
00:05:33,860 --> 00:05:39,400
repository only when you name it with
--git-dir or GIT_DIR, never because you

81
00:05:39,440 --> 00:05:41,284
happened to be inside one.

82
00:05:41,624 --> 00:05:46,660
The attack it answers. A bare repository
is a repository with no working tree: a

83
00:05:46,700 --> 00:05:51,672
directory with HEAD, objects, refs and
config, and it doesn't need to be called

84
00:05:51,712 --> 00:05:56,240
.git. A project can therefore contain
one as ordinary tracked files in a

85
00:05:56,280 --> 00:05:57,065
subdirectory.

86
00:05:57,405 --> 00:06:01,197
A clone does copy it, because to
Git those are ordinary files.

87
00:06:01,237 --> 00:06:06,390
If you then change into that subdirectory
and run any Git command, Git discovers the

88
00:06:06,430 --> 00:06:11,582
embedded bare repository and reads its
config. So this is a way around the rule that

89
00:06:11,622 --> 00:06:13,746
a clone doesn't copy configuration.

90
00:06:14,086 --> 00:06:18,918
The default in Git 2.x is all. explicit
will be the default in Git 3.0.

91
00:06:18,958 --> 00:06:24,271
The textbook's advice: if you don't work
inside bare repositories by hand, set

92
00:06:24,311 --> 00:06:25,781
explicit globally now.

93
00:06:26,121 --> 00:06:31,446
protocol.file.allow. In one sentence: since
2022 the file transport defaults to the

94
00:06:31,486 --> 00:06:36,812
policy user: you may use it directly, and
commands that start a clone on their own,

95
00:06:36,852 --> 00:06:42,242
such as submodule initialization, may not.
A submodule is another repository checked

96
00:06:42,282 --> 00:06:44,246
out in a subdirectory of yours.

97
00:06:44,586 --> 00:06:47,991
The setting has three values:
always, never and user.

98
00:06:48,031 --> 00:06:53,192
The safe network protocols default to
always, ext to never, and everything else,

99
00:06:53,232 --> 00:06:58,588
file included, to user. The risk it closes:
a .gitmodules file, the file that lists

100
00:06:58,628 --> 00:07:03,983
each submodule's path and URL, that names
a local path makes a recursive clone read

101
00:07:04,023 --> 00:07:07,494
from a directory on your disk
that the attacker chose.

102
00:07:07,834 --> 00:07:12,499
Recursive clones. The textbook says the
statement "cloning is safe" has had

103
00:07:12,539 --> 00:07:17,330
exceptions, and they share a shape: a
repository with submodules, cloned with

104
00:07:17,370 --> 00:07:22,412
--recurse-submodules, tricks Git into
writing a file where a hook is expected and

105
00:07:22,452 --> 00:07:27,180
then running it. Each has a CVE number,
its entry in the public catalogue of

106
00:07:27,220 --> 00:07:28,183
vulnerabilities.

107
00:07:28,523 --> 00:07:33,985
CVE-2024-32002, critical, fixed in
2.45.1 and backports in May 2024: on a

108
00:07:34,025 --> 00:07:40,315
case-insensitive filesystem with symbolic
links, a submodule could write a hook into

109
00:07:40,355 --> 00:07:45,816
.git that ran during the clone. The
textbook notes that macOS meets those

110
00:07:45,856 --> 00:07:52,071
conditions: its default filesystem is
case-insensitive and supports symbolic links.

111
00:07:52,411 --> 00:07:58,981
CVE-2025-48384, high, fixed in 2.50.1
and backports in July 2025: a trailing

112
00:07:59,021 --> 00:08:06,026
carriage return in a submodule path checked
content out to an unintended location

113
00:08:06,066 --> 00:08:08,027
where a hook could run.

114
00:08:08,367 --> 00:08:17,692
CVE-2025-48385, fixed in 2.50.1 and backports:
bundle URIs, which aren't enabled by

115
00:08:17,732 --> 00:08:18,594
default.

116
00:08:18,934 --> 00:08:26,217
And CVE-2024-52005, with the default changed
in core Git 2.55: unfiltered terminal

117
00:08:26,257 --> 00:08:29,254
escape sequences sent by a remote.

118
00:08:29,594 --> 00:08:34,761
Where you stand: no core-Git advisory
was published in 2026 up to the first of

119
00:08:34,801 --> 00:08:40,036
October. Your Git 2.55.0 contains every
published core fix, and so does Apple's

120
00:08:40,076 --> 00:08:45,577
2.50.1. And the advisory for the second CVE
names the standing workaround, which is

121
00:08:45,617 --> 00:08:50,851
the rule to keep: don't recursively clone
submodules of untrusted repositories.

122
00:08:51,191 --> 00:08:56,883
Hooks as a control. The manual documents 28
hooks, six of which run on the receiving

123
00:08:56,923 --> 00:09:02,479
side of a push. Client-side hooks aren't
cloned, and pre-commit and commit-msg are

124
00:09:02,519 --> 00:09:08,143
skipped by --no-verify. Since Git 2.54 hooks
can also be declared in configuration,

125
00:09:08,183 --> 00:09:13,602
including global and system configuration,
so the question "why did a hook run?"

126
00:09:13,642 --> 00:09:18,175
is now answered with git hook list
--show-scope and the event name.

127
00:09:18,514 --> 00:09:24,144
Quick quiz. A team wants to stop a certain
kind of commit, and installs a pre-commit

128
00:09:24,184 --> 00:09:29,340
hook on every laptop. Is that a control?
A, yes, it runs before every commit.

129
00:09:29,380 --> 00:09:32,445
B, no, the committer can
skip it. Your answer?

130
00:09:37,285 --> 00:09:42,118
B. A client-side hook isn't a control,
because the person it is meant to stop can

131
00:09:42,158 --> 00:09:42,599
skip it.

132
00:09:42,939 --> 00:09:48,180
Fake popularity. For an engineer who clones
research code weekly, the textbook says,

133
00:09:48,220 --> 00:09:53,398
the ecosystem is a larger risk than the
client. Researchers documented a network of

134
00:09:53,438 --> 00:09:58,552
more than 3,000 GitHub accounts that
distributed malware through repositories made

135
00:09:58,592 --> 00:10:03,707
to look popular. Hundreds of fake project
repositories that stole about 5 bitcoin.

136
00:10:03,747 --> 00:10:08,925
And about six million suspected fake stars.
Each figure is from the source cited in

137
00:10:08,965 --> 00:10:14,080
the section. The conclusion: stars, forks
and a polished README aren't evidence of

138
00:10:14,120 --> 00:10:14,772
legitimacy.

139
00:10:16,612 --> 00:10:21,791
Analogy, from the textbook. A clone is a
photocopy of a book: you get every page,

140
00:10:21,831 --> 00:10:26,946
and none of the previous owner's sticky
notes that say "when you open this, also

141
00:10:26,986 --> 00:10:32,166
call this number". An unpacked archive
that contains .git is the previous owner's

142
00:10:32,206 --> 00:10:35,646
own copy, sticky notes
included, and Git follows them.

143
00:10:35,986 --> 00:10:40,648
The analogy breaks in one place, and it
matters for an ML engineer more than for

144
00:10:40,688 --> 00:10:45,468
most. The pages can still contain code that
you later choose to run: a Makefile, a

145
00:10:45,508 --> 00:10:48,642
notebook. Git protects you
from Git running something.

146
00:10:48,682 --> 00:10:51,992
It doesn't protect you from
yourself running the project.

147
00:10:52,332 --> 00:10:55,038
So there are three rings
of trust, from inside out.

148
00:10:55,378 --> 00:11:01,024
Reading: git clone, git log, git show,
git diff. Git executes nothing the author

149
00:11:01,064 --> 00:11:01,450
chose.

150
00:11:01,790 --> 00:11:06,101
Git acting on the author's own metadata:
an unpacked .git, an embedded bare

151
00:11:06,141 --> 00:11:10,973
repository, a recursive clone. Here Git may
execute or be steered by what the author

152
00:11:11,013 --> 00:11:11,322
wrote.

153
00:11:11,661 --> 00:11:16,761
And running the project: pip install
-e ., make, a notebook's first cell.

154
00:11:16,801 --> 00:11:19,789
That's the author's
code, by your decision.

155
00:11:20,129 --> 00:11:23,717
The four rules of the section
follow from the rings.

156
00:11:23,757 --> 00:11:29,507
One: clone. Don't unpack somebody else's
.git. If you must, git clone --no-local it

157
00:11:29,547 --> 00:11:35,297
first. Two: treat a recursive clone of an
untrusted repository as running its code.

158
00:11:35,337 --> 00:11:39,204
Clone without submodules, read
.gitmodules, then decide.

159
00:11:39,244 --> 00:11:44,785
Three: set safe.bareRepository to explicit,
leave protocol.file.allow alone, and

160
00:11:44,825 --> 00:11:48,413
never set safe.directory to
a star on a workstation.

161
00:11:48,453 --> 00:11:54,133
Four: cloning is the safe part. Read before
you run, or run in a container without

162
00:11:54,173 --> 00:11:55,319
your credentials.

163
00:11:57,159 --> 00:12:02,230
Try it now, thirty seconds, on paper. Draw
a source repository as a box with three

164
00:12:02,270 --> 00:12:05,720
lines: objects and refs, the
config file, and the hooks.

165
00:12:05,760 --> 00:12:09,646
Draw one arrow labelled git clone,
and a second labelled unzip.

166
00:12:09,686 --> 00:12:13,635
For each arrow, which of the three
lines cross? Say it out loud.

167
00:12:18,475 --> 00:12:22,619
Check your arrows. With a clone,
one line crosses: objects and refs.

168
00:12:22,659 --> 00:12:26,495
Config and hooks stay behind.
With an archive, all three cross.

169
00:12:26,535 --> 00:12:31,110
That's the whole difference between the
two versions of the intern's story.

170
00:12:32,950 --> 00:12:34,779
Step 1: what the author has.

171
00:12:35,119 --> 00:12:39,584
A post-checkout hook that appends
a line to a log file inside .git.

172
00:12:39,624 --> 00:12:44,895
And an alias, st, whose value begins with
an exclamation mark: a shell command.

173
00:12:44,935 --> 00:12:46,912
Both harmless, both stand-ins.

174
00:12:47,252 --> 00:12:48,881
Step 2: clone it.

175
00:12:49,221 --> 00:12:53,307
git clone --no-local is 🟢 SAFE:
it creates a new repository.

176
00:12:53,347 --> 00:12:58,876
--no-local forces the normal transport
even for a path on the same disk, which is

177
00:12:58,916 --> 00:13:04,651
what the manual prescribes for untrusted
sources. git switch -c is 🟢 SAFE: it adds a

178
00:13:04,691 --> 00:13:10,289
ref. In the clone: how many active hooks,
is the alias defined, and does switching

179
00:13:10,329 --> 00:13:12,971
branches run the hook? Say it out loud.

180
00:13:17,811 --> 00:13:21,933
Zero active hooks. The alias
lookup exits with 1: not defined.

181
00:13:21,973 --> 00:13:24,685
And after a branch switch: "no hook ran".

182
00:13:25,025 --> 00:13:27,328
Step 3: receive the same
repository as an archive.

183
00:13:27,668 --> 00:13:33,197
cp -R stands for what unpacking a zip or
a tarball that includes .git gives you:

184
00:13:33,237 --> 00:13:37,304
every file under .git arrives
as written. Two Git commands.

185
00:13:37,344 --> 00:13:40,159
What does each run? Make your prediction.

186
00:13:44,999 --> 00:13:49,160
git st printed the author's sentence:
it ran the author's shell command.

187
00:13:49,200 --> 00:13:52,720
git switch ran the author's hook,
and the log file proves it.

188
00:13:52,760 --> 00:13:57,447
That's the intern's second story, with a
harmless sentence in place of a program.

189
00:13:57,487 --> 00:14:01,707
With a hostile archive those would
have been any program, running as you.

190
00:14:02,047 --> 00:14:04,181
Step 4: dubious ownership.

191
00:14:04,521 --> 00:14:09,324
The sandbox can't change file ownership
without sudo, so the transcript uses the

192
00:14:09,364 --> 00:14:13,441
variable that Git's own test suite
uses to simulate a foreign owner.

193
00:14:13,481 --> 00:14:18,465
The real message appears in containers and
CI, where the checkout is often owned by

194
00:14:18,505 --> 00:14:19,434
another user ID.

195
00:14:19,774 --> 00:14:24,902
"Detected dubious ownership", exit status
128, and Git prints the command for an

196
00:14:24,942 --> 00:14:27,034
exception for this one directory.

197
00:14:27,374 --> 00:14:32,885
git config set --global --append safe.directory
is 🟡 CAUTION: it makes Git trust

198
00:14:32,925 --> 00:14:38,506
that directory's configuration and hooks.
The preview is to read that directory's

199
00:14:38,546 --> 00:14:43,433
.git/config and hooks first. After
the exception is removed from global

200
00:14:43,473 --> 00:14:48,499
configuration, a star is written into
the repository's own configuration.

201
00:14:48,539 --> 00:14:52,176
Does Git accept the repository
then? Say it out loud.

202
00:14:57,016 --> 00:15:00,484
With the exception in global
configuration, status works.

203
00:15:00,524 --> 00:15:05,099
With a star in the repository's own
configuration: dubious ownership again.

204
00:15:05,139 --> 00:15:08,730
The textbook calls the last
three lines the important ones.

205
00:15:08,770 --> 00:15:13,530
That file is exactly what Git declined
to trust, so writing "trust me" into it

206
00:15:13,570 --> 00:15:17,653
changes nothing. Almost everyone
expects it to work the first time.

207
00:15:17,993 --> 00:15:20,515
Step 5: an embedded bare repository.

208
00:15:20,855 --> 00:15:25,938
git config set --global safe.bareRepository
explicit is 🟡 CAUTION: global

209
00:15:25,978 --> 00:15:31,552
configuration keeps no history, and the
setting changes how later commands treat

210
00:15:31,592 --> 00:15:32,815
bare repositories.

211
00:15:33,155 --> 00:15:38,056
By default, changing into the bare
repository is enough for Git to use it.

212
00:15:38,096 --> 00:15:41,861
With explicit, Git refuses:
"cannot use bare repository".

213
00:15:41,901 --> 00:15:47,403
Naming it with --git-dir works. The script
unsets the value again, because the labs

214
00:15:47,443 --> 00:15:49,940
inspect the bare "server" with git -C.

215
00:15:50,280 --> 00:15:52,792
Step 6: a clone you did not ask for.

216
00:15:53,132 --> 00:15:58,075
A submodule whose URL is a local path.
The setting isn't configured at all.

217
00:15:58,115 --> 00:16:00,400
What happens? Make your prediction.

218
00:16:05,240 --> 00:16:10,083
"Transport 'file' not allowed." The default
policy let you use the file transport

219
00:16:10,123 --> 00:16:13,881
yourself and refused it to a clone
that Git started on its own.

220
00:16:13,921 --> 00:16:18,763
The one-off -c on the command line is
correct for a local experiment such as this

221
00:16:18,803 --> 00:16:22,500
one. Setting it globally removes
the guard. Leave the default.

222
00:16:24,340 --> 00:16:25,988
Five mistakes to watch for.

223
00:16:26,328 --> 00:16:29,655
Unpacking an archive that
contains .git and working in it.

224
00:16:29,695 --> 00:16:34,066
Root cause: every file under .git arrives
as written, including the author's

225
00:16:34,106 --> 00:16:36,735
configuration and hooks,
and Git follows them.

226
00:16:37,075 --> 00:16:42,260
Setting safe.directory to a star. Root
cause: that switches the ownership check off

227
00:16:42,300 --> 00:16:47,359
for every repository on the machine; list
the one directory or fix the ownership.

228
00:16:47,699 --> 00:16:51,221
Cloning an untrusted repository
with --recurse-submodules.

229
00:16:51,261 --> 00:16:56,072
Root cause: the published exceptions to
"cloning is safe" share that shape; the

230
00:16:56,112 --> 00:16:58,283
standing workaround is not to do it.

231
00:16:58,623 --> 00:17:03,642
Relying on a client-side hook as a control.
Root cause: hooks are not cloned, and

232
00:17:03,682 --> 00:17:07,702
the person they are meant to stop
can skip them with --no-verify.

233
00:17:08,042 --> 00:17:12,773
Trusting a repository because it has many
stars. Root cause: stars, forks and a

234
00:17:12,813 --> 00:17:17,060
polished README can be manufactured
and are not evidence of legitimacy.

235
00:17:18,900 --> 00:17:24,129
Now, out of the lab. The textbook's case.
An ML team receives a "reproduction

236
00:17:24,169 --> 00:17:28,646
package" for a paper as a tarball
that contains a full repository.

237
00:17:28,686 --> 00:17:34,394
A shell prompt that shows the branch name
runs git status as soon as someone changes

238
00:17:34,434 --> 00:17:39,936
into the directory. And git status consults
core.fsmonitor from the package's own

239
00:17:39,976 --> 00:17:40,758
.git/config.

240
00:17:41,098 --> 00:17:44,438
Nobody typed a Git command.
Nobody ran the project.

241
00:17:44,478 --> 00:17:46,426
Changing directory was enough.

242
00:17:46,766 --> 00:17:51,768
The safe procedure is the manual's: git
clone --no-local package clean, and work in

243
00:17:51,808 --> 00:17:56,141
clean. The clone takes the objects
and the refs, and leaves the author's

244
00:17:56,181 --> 00:18:01,122
configuration and hooks behind. After that
the team is in the first ring: reading.

245
00:18:01,162 --> 00:18:05,982
Whether to enter the third ring and run
the paper's code is a separate decision,

246
00:18:06,022 --> 00:18:10,051
made after reading, or made inside
a container without credentials.

247
00:18:11,891 --> 00:18:17,462
Your turn. Do Exercise 30.1,
"What could that have run?", in

248
00:18:17,502 --> 00:18:21,016
exercises/m26-m31-actions-security.md.

249
00:18:21,356 --> 00:18:26,131
For each situation in the exercise, before
you answer, say which of the three rings

250
00:18:26,171 --> 00:18:30,713
it is in: reading, Git acting on the
author's metadata, or running the project.

251
00:18:30,753 --> 00:18:34,658
Predict for each which file, if any,
could cause a program to start.

252
00:18:34,998 --> 00:18:39,878
The challenge is Exercise 30.7, "The
reproduction package", in the same file.

253
00:18:41,718 --> 00:18:44,934
Read the question on screen.
Say your answer out loud.

254
00:18:49,774 --> 00:18:54,405
A strong answer names the two kinds of
file under .git that can make Git start a

255
00:18:54,445 --> 00:18:59,076
program, with examples of each, and says
what a clone creates in their place and

256
00:18:59,116 --> 00:19:03,923
from where. It then describes what an
unpacked copy contains and gives one ordinary

257
00:19:03,963 --> 00:19:08,595
command that would trigger each kind. It
names the documented way to get a clean

258
00:19:08,635 --> 00:19:13,266
copy. The follow-up says that "cloning is
safe" has had exceptions and asks what

259
00:19:13,306 --> 00:19:17,646
shape they share and what standing rule
follows. You have the table and the

260
00:19:17,686 --> 00:19:18,288
workaround.

261
00:19:20,128 --> 00:19:21,055
Let's land this.

262
00:19:21,395 --> 00:19:23,055
You should now be able to say:

263
00:19:23,395 --> 00:19:28,546
A clone copies objects and refs; hooks and
configuration are not copied, so cloning

264
00:19:28,586 --> 00:19:30,422
and reading is generally safe.

265
00:19:30,762 --> 00:19:35,994
An unpacked .git brings the author's
configuration and hooks; get a clean copy with

266
00:19:36,034 --> 00:19:37,328
git clone --no-local.

267
00:19:37,668 --> 00:19:42,885
safe.directory is the exception list for
repositories owned by another user, honored

268
00:19:42,925 --> 00:19:47,891
only in protected configuration, and never
to be set to a star on a workstation.

269
00:19:48,231 --> 00:19:53,720
safe.bareRepository=explicit refuses
bare repositories that were not named

270
00:19:53,760 --> 00:19:59,995
explicitly, and protocol.file.allow keeps
Git from starting file-transport clones on

271
00:20:00,035 --> 00:20:00,593
its own.

272
00:20:00,933 --> 00:20:05,769
A recursive clone of an untrusted repository
is to be treated as running its code,

273
00:20:05,809 --> 00:20:07,552
and popularity proves nothing.

274
00:20:09,392 --> 00:20:14,759
Read sections 21B.2 to
21B.4 of Chapter 21B.

275
00:20:15,099 --> 00:20:19,957
Today you watched the same repository
arrive twice, as a clone and as an archive,

276
00:20:19,997 --> 00:20:24,916
and you know which one runs the author's
files. Replay that demo in the lab shell.

277
00:20:24,956 --> 00:20:28,302
Next time: credentials, and
what a stolen one can reach.

278
00:20:28,342 --> 00:20:31,446
Until then, look at the
state first and type second.

279
00:20:31,486 --> 00:20:32,898
See you in the next one.
